[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Li et al., 2021-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1ef3f771d5986e40966e9d3e6206a617ddf64fef)
- venue: PLoS ONE
- citation count: 2
[method]
The paper describes a K-means clustering evaluation method based on the silhouette coefficient (SC). For each sample i, the SC is calculated from a(i) and b(i) using the formula (b(i) - a(i)) / max(a(i), b(i)), with piecewise forms: 1 - a(i)/b(i) when a(i) < b(i), 0 when a(i) = b(i), and b(i)/a(i) - 1 when a(i) > b(i). The mean SC is then obtained by averaging the SC values across all samples. This mean SC is used as a clustering quality metric, and the optimal number of K-means clusters is selected by maximizing the mean SC. This relates to the proposed research method because the plan uses k-means clustering with k = 2 to segregate mice into susceptible and unsusceptible memory phenotypes; the paper’s silhouette coefficient approach is relevant as a possible metric for evaluating whether a two-cluster solution is appropriate, although the paper does not apply it to mouse behavioral data.
[results]
No quantitative clustering outcome, dataset performance value, baseline comparison, or statistical significance is reported in the provided excerpt. The only reported outcome is the stated decision rule that a larger mean silhouette coefficient indicates better clustering performance and that the optimal number of K-means clusters should be determined by maximizing the mean SC.
[context]
The method is computational and does not specify a biological dataset, sample size, hardware, software, or hyperparameters. The silhouette coefficient is constrained to the range [-1, 1], and the clustering decision is based on the average SC across all samples. The excerpt does not provide the actual values of a(i), b(i), the number of clusters tested, the data used, or any experimental conditions beyond the mathematical definition of the silhouette coefficient.
[end related work 1]

