- in_text_citation: [(Li et al., 2015-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/da3451e64649704450ce6b37240c4ce899065256)
- venue: Journal of Neuroinflammation
- citation count: 53
[method]
The paper used cultured sensory neurons to test whether S1P-induced sensitization depends on S1PR1 and S1PR3. Neurons were isolated and maintained, treated with siRNAs targeting S1PR1/2/3 or S1PR1/3, and S1PR expression was assessed by TaqMan qPCR. Functional effects were measured by electrophysiology after exposure to S1P, the S1PR3 agonist CYM-5541, and S1PR1/S1PR3 antagonists VPC23019 and VPC44116. This is related to the proposed method because both use pharmacological modulation of S1PR signaling to test pain-related outcomes, although the paper tests sensory neuron excitability in vitro rather than DG-targeted SEW2871 in CCI mice.
[results]
The section headings report that siRNAs effectively and specifically knocked down S1PR expression. Pooled siRNAs targeting S1PR1/2/3 or S1PR1/3 blocked the S1P-induced increase in excitability. The selective S1PR3 agonist CYM-5541 sensitized sensory neurons. Selective antagonists at S1PR1 and S1PR3 abolished S1P-induced sensitization, and VPC23019 and VPC44116, antagonists at both S1PR1 and S1PR3, blocked S1P-induced sensitization. S1P did not activate a membrane current in sensory neurons. No quantitative values, effect sizes, or statistical tests are available in the supplied text.
[context]
The experiments were performed in an in vitro sensory neuron preparation, not in vivo in the dentate gyrus, CCI model, or memory tasks. The available text does not specify cell type details, siRNA sequences, dosing, exposure durations, electrophysiological metrics, sample sizes, or statistical thresholds. The relevance to the proposed research plan is that it supports S1PR1/S1PR3-dependent modulation of pain-related neuronal excitability, but it does not directly validate DG-localized S1PR1 agonism, SEW2871, chronic pain-related memory impairment, or susceptible/unsusceptible phenotypes.
[end related work 1]

