- in_text_citation: [(Mahmmoud et al., 2015-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/32ca6b4fe38e446ebaf9a2a0cf855c34978e2a0b)
- venue: PLoS ONE
- citation count: 141
[method]
The paper combined radial arm maze spatial memory training with hippocampal dendritic morphometry to assess structural plasticity. Rats were adapted for 5 days, maintained at approximately 85% of free-feeding weight, habituated to a 12-arm maze, and trained for 10 daily sessions of up to 8 minutes. Eight arms were baited and four arms were unbaited in a fixed pattern for each rat; repeated entries into baited arms were scored as working memory errors, and entries into unbaited arms were scored as reference memory errors. Untrained controls spent the same time in the maze with unbaited arms. Six hours after the final training session, animals were perfused with 1.5% paraformaldehyde, brains were processed, and 200 µm sections were prepared for dendritic morphometry and spine density quantification in CA1 and CA3 apical and basal dendrites. This method is related to the proposed structural plasticity approach because it uses hippocampal dendritic/spine morphology to link structural changes with spatial memory performance, but it differs from the proposed method by focusing on CA1/CA3 spine density rather than DG-specific TEM quantification of excitatory synapses, postsynaptic density dimensions, or Golgi-based dendritic complexity and spine subtype analysis.
[results]
The supplied text reports that rats showed improved performance in the radial arm maze and that spine density was increased in apical and basal dendrites of CA3 following radial arm maze performance. It also reports that CA1 basal dendritic spines were negatively correlated with working memory errors, and that CA3 basal dendritic spines were negatively correlated with working memory errors and latency. No numerical values, effect sizes, group sizes for the morphometric outcomes, or statistical significance values are provided in the supplied excerpt.
[context]
Key experimental conditions included a 12-arm radial arm maze with a 50 cm central platform and 12 cm by 60 cm arms, eight baited arms per rat, four unbaited arms, 10 training sessions, an 8-minute session limit, water deprivation to approximately 85% free-feeding weight, video tracking, and untrained unbaited controls. Tissue processing used 1.5% paraformaldehyde perfusion and 200 µm sections; the supplied perfusion section states eight animals per group but inconsistently refers to mice after describing rats. The paper's morphological analysis was limited to CA1 and CA3 spine density and did not report DG-specific dendritic complexity, total dendritic length, spine subtype classification, excitatory synapse density per unit area, or postsynaptic density thickness or length, so it provides only partial support for the proposed DG structural plasticity assays.
[end related work 1]

