  "support": "The strongest support for the proposed actin-cytoskeleton component comes from [(Wu et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8e083cd920f9ae18b2d3222b7c16d82a0da0722f), which used a rat SNL neuropathic pain model and directly measured F-actin/G-actin ratio, Rac1 activation, and Golgi dendritic spine morphology in the spinal dorsal horn. This is the closest methodological precedent to the proposed plan's F/G ratio, Rac1 analysis, and structural spine readouts in chronic pain, and it shows that neuropathic injury produces time-dependent changes in srGAP3, Rac1 activity, and F/G ratio, with electroacupuncture reducing Rac1 activity, p-NR2B, PSD95, and F/G ratio while CN04 blocked those effects. Although the tissue is spinal dorsal horn rather than mouse DG and the study does not address S1PR1, Y2H, co-IP, Cdc42, Arp2/3, or ITGA2, it supports the feasibility and relevance of using F/G and Rac1 as pain-related actin endpoints. The next strongest support is [(Das et al., 2017-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eb6af7fd4cae03b2257808ca5a99925b24362870), which used AAV-mediated Rac1 activation in the lateral amygdala and PAK inhibitor IPA3 versus PIR-3.5 in mouse fear conditioning, showing that Rac1 activation during training impaired long-term fear memory and PAK inhibition enhanced long-term memory. This supports the proposed use of Rac1 as a memory-relevant actin regulator and the broader use of AAV-based manipulation in memory circuits, but it does not validate S1PR1, DG specificity, chronic pain, F/G ratios, or interaction assays. The weakest but still relevant support is [(Wu et al., 2019-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3ba9174d81d903f1f69fc067d70808dd8150af29), which used Western blotting to show enhanced Rac1 activity in Alzheimer's disease patients and animal models and identified compounds that inhibit Rac1 activity in memory-related behavioral screening. This supports the proposed Western blotting of Rac1 in a memory