  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction or limiting evidence is that [(Wu et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8e083cd920f9ae18b2d3222b7c16d82a0da0722f) reports biphasic post-SNL changes: srGAP3 increased early then decreased later, while Rac1 activity and F/G ratio decreased early then increased later. This contradicts any assumption that a single 28-day post-CCI measurement in the DG will capture a stable or unidirectional actin phenotype; if the proposed method samples only late chronic pain, it may detect increased Rac1/F-G, whereas early or treatment-responsive states may show the opposite. The same study also shows that electroacupuncture improved pain threshold and inhibited spine remodeling while decreasing Rac1, p-NR2B, PSD95, and F/G, so a proposed S1PR1 agonist that increases Rac1/F-G could be maladaptive unless the method specifies the expected direction. The study localizes these changes to spinal dorsal horn, so it does not support direct extrapolation to DG without DG-specific validation. The next contradiction is from [(Das et al., 2017-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eb6af7fd4cae03b2257808ca5a99925b24362870), which shows that Rac1 effects are timing- and effector-dependent: Rac1 activation during training impaired long-term fear memory, but activation one day after training had no effect, and PAK inhibition enhanced long-term memory. This contradicts a simplified model in which S1PR1-driven Rac1 expression or activity alone determines memory vulnerability; it suggests that downstream PAK signaling and the temporal window of Rac1 activation are critical, yet the proposed method does not measure PAK, srGAP3, or multiple time points. The weakest contradiction is that [(Wu et al., 2019-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3ba9174d81d903f1f69fc067d70808dd8150af29), [(Das et al., 2017-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eb6af7fd4cae03b2257808ca5a99925b24362870), and [(Wu et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8e083cd920f9ae18b2d3222b7c16d82a0da0722f) do not directly test S1PR1, ITGA2, Cdc42, Arp2/3, yeast two-hybrid screening, or co-IP in DG tissue, so the proposed S1PR1 interaction screen is not directly supported by this evidence base and may identify indirect or non-specific binders if not constrained by S1PR1-specific controls.",
  "suggested_action": "Most important, revise the mechanistic timeline to include early, intermediate, and late post-CCI time points, for example 3-7, 14, and 28 days, for DG F/G ratio, Rac1 activity, Cdc42, Arp2/3, ITGA2, PAK phosphorylation, and srGAP3, because [(Wu et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8e083cd920f9ae18b2d3222b7c16d82a0da0722f) shows biphasic Rac1/F-G changes after neuropathic injury and a single 28-day snapshot may miss the relevant susceptible or unsusceptible molecular state. Second, add PAK and srGAP3 readouts and, if feasible, a PAK inhibitor or Rac1 dominant-negative or constitutively active AAV manipulation in the DG, because [(Das et al., 2017-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eb6af7fd4cae03b2257808ca5a99925b24362870) indicates that Rac1 memory effects depend on timing and downstream PAK signaling rather than Rac1 alone; when testing SEW2871, predefine whether increased or decreased Rac1/F-G is expected to be protective and test whether the effect is Rac1/PAK-dependent. Third, make the S1PR1 interaction screen more rigorous by using S1PR1-specific baits, negative-control baits, mass-spectrometry or orthogonal pull-down validation, and co-IP or proximity ligation in DG tissue, because none of the related work directly supports S1PR1 binding to Rac1, Cdc42, Arp2/3, or ITGA2. Fourth, couple the actin and interaction data to DG-specific structural phenotypes by quantifying Golgi spine density, spine type, dendritic length, and TEM PSD or excitatory synapse counts in susceptible versus unsusceptible mice, as supported by the spine-remodeling evidence in [(Wu et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8e083cd920f9ae18b2d3222b7c16d82a0da0722f). Fifth, in HT-22 hippocampal neuronal cells, combine phalloidin staining with S1PR1 agonist or antagonist treatment and Rac1 or PAK manipulation to test whether S1PR1 signaling directly changes actin polymerization, because the related work supports actin readouts but